    "practice": "What does drift concern?|Observed remote divergence|Only an address rename with an unchanged remote object and no changed attributes|Only a source formatting change that leaves every remote property identical|Only a formatting change|Drift concerns observed remote changes relative to intended and tracked infrastructure.\nWhat does an import establish?|An object-address binding|Guaranteed configuration compatibility and a harmless subsequent plan|Automatic authorization to take ownership from the existing infrastructure team|A cloud-wide inventory deletion|Import creates a managed address binding but still requires configuration review.\nWhat does a moved block communicate?|An intended address migration|A new encryption algorithm|A mandatory resource replacement|A billing discount|Moved blocks preserve continuity across supported address refactors.\nAn emergency capacity edit appears in a plan. What should happen first?|Check intended policy|Restore old capacity immediately because every out-of-band change is necessarily wrong|Adopt every console change permanently without checking its owner or purpose|Assume every external edit is malicious|Drift decisions need operational intent and ownership context.\nA renamed resource would otherwise appear as delete and create. What may preserve identity?|A valid reviewed moved block|Only renaming the tf file|Only changing indentation|Only marking an output sensitive|Address migration metadata can retain the existing object binding.\nThe fixture preserves i-7 through a key change. What is its limit?|It does not execute Terraform state migration|It proves provider move compatibility|It proves every remote object is healthy|It tests production backup restoration|The dictionary example demonstrates logical identity continuity only.",
